<task id="040" project="ai-advanced-capabilities-agreements-r1">
  <metadata>
    <title>#2 Bidirectional highlight — summary row → document section AND gutter Review Note</title>
    <phase>4 Review-Depth UX</phase>
    <gate>blocked (deps 011)</gate>
    <status>completed</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Compose shared-lib .tsx changes (auto-FULL); cross-pane interaction correctness.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>sonnet</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Small, well-specified UX link over an existing join key.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <parallel-group>E</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>true</parallel-safe>
    <deps>011</deps>
    <tags>compose, frontend, fluent-ui</tags>
    <estimated-effort>2-3 hours</estimated-effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>
    Selecting a Review Summary row today highlights the document section but NOT the matching gutter Review Note. Add
    the reverse link: row selection also scrolls to + highlights the matching gutter note. Join key = the finding's
    computed clause number / paraId anchor (deterministic since 011).
  </prompt>
  <role>SPAARKE platform developer. Compose panel/gutter interaction, Fluent v9.</role>
  <goal>One click on a summary row lights up BOTH the clause in the document AND its gutter note.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      AgreementReviewSummaryPanel rows already resolve docPosition (doc→highlight shipped in nda-r1). The gutter
      (ComposeCommentGutter) renders notes anchored by thread/paraId. Add: row select → locate the gutter note by the
      shared anchor (011's paraId/ComputedNumber) → scroll + transient highlight (same visual language as the doc-side
      highlight). Keep it ADR-030-typed if it crosses panes (panel and gutter are both Compose-side — likely a direct
      callback/context, not a bus event; decide at the seam).
    </background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="modify">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/AgreementReviewSummaryPanel.tsx</file>
      <file role="modify">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/ComposeCommentGutter.tsx</file>
    </relevant-files>
    <dependencies>
      <dependency task="011" status="pending">stable paraId/ComputedNumber join key</dependency>
    </dependencies>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="ADR-021">Highlight styling uses Fluent v9 semantic tokens (works in dark mode); hover/pointer affordances only on clickable regions. Scope: new UI states.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">The reverse link must NOT steal focus from the editor or scroll the document a second time — one coordinated highlight action. Scope: the interaction.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <topic>Compose panel/gutter UX</topic>
    <files>
      <file>.claude/adr/ADR-021-fluent-design-system.md</file>
      <file>.claude/adr/ADR-030-pane-event-bus.md</file>
      <file>.claude/patterns/ui/fluent-v9-component-authoring.md</file>
    </files>
    <patterns>
      <pattern name="doc-side highlight" location="src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/AgreementReviewSummaryPanel.tsx (docPosition resolution)">Mirror the existing doc-highlight mechanism for the gutter side.</pattern>
    </patterns>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Declare rigor; trace the existing row→doc highlight flow + the gutter's note-identity model.</step>
    <step order="1">Implement row→gutter-note resolution via the shared anchor; scroll + highlight with the doc-side visual language.</step>
    <step order="2">Tests: matched note highlights; unmatched (note deleted) degrades gracefully; no double-scroll.</step>
    <step order="3">Update TASK-INDEX.md: 040 ✅.</step>
  </steps>

  <ui-tests>
    <test name="Bidirectional highlight"><steps><step>Run a review; open the summary panel</step><step>Click a row</step></steps><expected>Document section AND its gutter note both highlight; gutter scrolls the note into view.</expected></test>
    <test name="Dark mode"><steps><step>Toggle dark mode; click a row</step></steps><expected>Highlight colors from semantic tokens; readable in both themes.</expected></test>
  </ui-tests>

  <tools><tool name="npm">lib build + tests</tool></tools>
  <outputs><output type="code">panel + gutter (reverse link)</output><output type="test">interaction tests</output></outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">Selecting a summary row highlights BOTH the doc section and the matching gutter note (scrolled into view).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A row whose note was removed highlights the doc only + no error (graceful).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative: rapid row-switching leaves exactly one highlighted pair (no stacking).</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Spec FR-10; design Lens 2 #2.

  COMPLETED (sonnet @ high, directional). Traced the actual host: `ComposeEditor.tsx` (not
  `ComposeWorkspace.tsx`) mounts both `AgreementReviewSummaryPanel` and `ComposeCommentGutter` and already
  owns the `selectedThreadId`/`selectThread` shared-highlight mechanism (UAT round-4 #8). Added
  `resolveMatchingThreadId(finding, threads)` as a pure, unit-tested export in `ComposeCommentGutter.tsx` —
  the deterministic join on `sectionRef` (tie-broken by `explanation`), never a guess on ambiguity (mirrors
  DEF-01 discipline). Wired a ~25-line additive call-site change in `ComposeEditor.tsx`'s
  `handleReviewNavigate`: on a match, calls the existing `selectThread` (now with an additive
  `forceSelect` param) instead of the doc-only `qaHighlight` — ONE coordinated action, no double scroll,
  no focus steal, same yellow "selected" visual language the gutter-card click already uses. Falls back to
  the pre-existing doc-only ephemeral highlight when unmatched (placement failure / removed note) —
  graceful degrade. Tests: 6 new pure-function cases in `ComposeCommentGutter.test.tsx` + new
  `ComposeEditor.bidirectionalHighlight.test.tsx` (3 integration tests via DOM decoration classes, since
  gutter-card positioning needs `coordsAtPos` which isn't reliably reachable in jsdom without spying on the
  internal editor). Build clean; full package suite 795/810 passed (15 pre-existing failures, exact
  named set, zero new regressions); all 14 `ComposeEditor.*` suites (63 tests) green including the
  untouched DEF-01 `advisoryComments` suite. One disclosed deviation: touched `ComposeEditor.tsx` (not in
  the forbidden list, not task-030-owned this wave) because it is the only file holding both the findings
  and the threads — see `notes/040-execution-notes.md` for the full seam-decision writeup.
  </notes>
  <execution><skill>.claude/skills/task-execute/SKILL.md</skill></execution>
</task>
